[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged on subject lines and marked known: one bug, one fix
queued. Its reports hold three problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first one.
Each problem now has its own ticket.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser does not close #4471. The other two problems keep sending reports until
their own fixes ship.
</Callout>

## `parser` · the shared parser reads every date month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads month first, so day and
month swap: 4 March becomes 3 April. Reports come through every channel. Intake tagged
some of these `hint` from the reporter's browser; the tenant decides the cause, not the browser.

## `hint` · booking-v3 tells day-first people to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. The tenant's people type what the hint
says, and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. Only reports sent through the
form of a booking-v3 tenant belong here. The parser fix does not touch it: the form needs a
hint in the tenant's own order.

## `shift` · the date moves by one day

These reports describe no swap. The date moves one day earlier, and it
depends on where the reader is: a client in Lisbon saw Tuesday, a client in Toronto saw Monday,
for the same meeting. One invite arrived before its booking existed. This looks like a date
converted across time zones at midnight. The desk read these as a one-day move
([desk notes](https://github.com/SaraGenially/support-kb/blob/main/src/lib/data/desk-notes.json)),
but the #4471 table still counts them under `parser` by tenant.

## How this was counted

- One id, one report: the export pages overlap.
- The cause comes from the tenant (locale and booking form) and the channel, never from the
  intake tag.
- The one-day moves come from the desk's own notes, checked against each report's text.

## Lessons

- A subject line says how a problem looks, not what causes it. Read the bodies before a big
  ticket is marked known.
- When a fix ships, check which reports it really covers.

